        "Alexandre Blais",
        "Andreas Wallraff",
        "Robert J. Schoelkopf"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Circuit QED made superconducting artificial atoms a controllable platform for quantum optics and quantum information, opening a major experimental regime and underpinning a leading approach to quantum computing.",
        "attribution": "Blais supplied a key theoretical architecture, while Wallraff and Schoelkopf led landmark experimental demonstrations and platform development. Steven M. Girvin's theoretical contributions are also central, making any three-person allocation difficult.",
        "maturity": "The framework has been validated across many laboratories and devices over two decades; quantum computing applications continue to develop, but the foundational physics is established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery spans theory and experiment with overlapping contributions, so the proposed three names may not capture all essential credit."
        ]
      }
    },
